Position Overview:
Responsible to load stock material and run computer numeric controlled (CNC) machines (i.e. multi-axis milling machines or lathes) to produce all types of aircraft and aerospace components, sub-assemblies and final assemblies in accordance with customer or engineering specifications, Company procedures, SWIMS, etc.

What You Will Do:
• Interact with Supervisor and/or Charge Hand by communicating and receiving instruction in the transition of the workflow and production work scheduling; to assess current material needs and/or to address any problems accordingly.

• Communicate all production related items to the next shift operators or shift supervisor.

• Adhere to workplace safety policies, regulations and the use safety equipment will be the highest priority for you and your co-workers;

• Work initially with CNC Machinist (Set-up) to receive instructions on loading stock material, running parts and preparing a test run to ensure machine is working properly.

• Work with CNC Machinist (Set-up) when referring to process sheets, blueprints and/or part sketches to determine machining requirements, dimensional specifications, set-up and/or operating requirements;

• Work closely with CNC Machinist and Supervisor (or Charge Hand) to resolve machine run and/or programming issues in order to produce better machining results;

• Load and unload raw materials and equipment;

• Oversee applicable CNC machine (or multiple machines) while machine(s) executes the machining task and prepare/set-up next job preparation;

• Deburr and inspect parts to assure conformance to CNC machine's set-up sheet, blueprints, customer specifications, Company workplace instructions, SWIMS, manuals, etc.;

• Ensure effective production of quality parts by checking parts to drawings, sketches, etc., while visually checking every part and using precision measuring equipment instruments to measure critical dimensions to ensure quality;

• Perform routine daily maintenance and cleaning, help troubleshoot CNC Machine for problems and/or clean equipment accordingly;

• Participate in Continuous Improvement, Lean and 8S activities in your area.

What You Will Bring:
• Completion of High School (Grade 12), equivalent experience and/or training.

• Must possess mechanical aptitude, high attention to detail, a high level of manual dexterity and a strong interest in aircraft assembly and/or repair;

• Completion of Global Harmonized System (WHMIS) course.

• Must be able to work with others co-operatively and follow directives precisely.

• Knowledge of processes, products and equipment and ability to read blueprints and engineering documentation will be considered an asset.

• Must meet physical requirements of the position including but not limited to: walking, static standing and leg/foot movement for extended periods, bending, stooping, squatting, twisting/trunk rotation, weight bearing / lifting, carrying, pushing & pulling heavy objects; hand/wrist gripping, grasping, pinching, vibration depend on tasks/tools, neck & back flexion.

• Adhere to, and demonstrate a personal commitment to IMP Occupational Health, Safety and Environment Policies. Continually focusing on your individual safety and the safety of others who may be affected by your work and to fully comply with all Nova Scotia Provincial Health, Safety and Environment Legislation.

• Must be eligible to meet the requirements of the Canadian Controlled Goods Program (CGP) and the Canadian Security Clearances – Secret Level II

